<%- include('partials/header') %>

<div class="container privacy-page" style="max-width: 880px; margin: 40px auto 60px;">
  <div class="page-header" style="margin-bottom: 32px;">
    <div>
      <h1>GitPR Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="subtitle" style="font-size: 1.05rem;">
        Plain-language explanations of how GitPR handles code diffs, API keys, style rules, and data deletion.
      </p>
      <span style="font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--text-muted); display: inline-block; margin-top: 6px;">Last updated: November 2026</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" style="padding: 36px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 32px;">
    <!-- Section 1 -->
    <section>
      <h2 style="font-size: 1.3rem; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; color: var(--text-primary);">
        <span class="badge badge-info">1</span> Code Diffs & LLM Processing
      </h2>
      <p style="color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7;">
        When you open or update a pull request, GitPR receives a webhook from GitHub containing the changed file diffs.
      </p>
      <ul style="margin: 12px 0 12px 24px; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8;">
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Ephemeral Diff Transmission:</strong> Diffs are sent directly to the Google Gemini API to analyze for bugs and security vulnerabilities.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Zero Permanent Code Storage:</strong> GitPR does <em>not</em> store your source code or diffs in our database or on disk. Once the review is posted, diffs are immediately discarded from memory.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Model Training Protection:</strong> Prompts sent through the Google GenAI API with your API key are governed by Google's API data terms and are not used to train or improve foundational models.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Prompt Injection Defense:</strong> Diffs and team style guide rules are strictly categorized as <em>untrusted data</em> in our system prompt. Instructions contained inside code or comments cannot override bot controls.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2 -->
    <section style="border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); padding-top: 28px;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 1.3rem; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; color: var(--text-primary);">
        <span class="badge badge-success">2</span> Bring-Your-Own-Key (BYOK) Encryption
      </h2>
      <p style="color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7;">
        GitPR operates on a Bring-Your-Own-Key model for Google Gemini. You provide your own free Gemini API key, ensuring you maintain complete control over your API quotas and access.
      </p>
      <ul style="margin: 12px 0 12px 24px; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8;">
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">AES-256-GCM Authenticated Encryption:</strong> Your API key is encrypted using AES-256-GCM with a unique, cryptographically random 96-bit initialization vector (IV) and an authentication tag.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Isolated Master Key:</strong> The master encryption key is provided via the server's <code>ENCRYPTION_KEY</code> environment variable. It is never stored in the database or committed to source control.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">In-Memory Decryption Only:</strong> Keys are decrypted exclusively in-memory inside the background review worker for the single PR being reviewed, and are immediately discarded after the review comments are posted.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Last 4 Characters Visible:</strong> Neither our UI nor our logs ever reveal your full API key. Only the last 4 characters (e.g. <code>•••• •••• •••• 1234</code>) are displayed for identification.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Immediate Deletion:</strong> You can rotate or permanently delete your stored API key at any time from the <a href="/settings" style="color: #818cf8;">Settings</a> page.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 3 -->
    <section style="border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); padding-top: 28px;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 1.3rem; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; color: var(--text-primary);">
        <span class="badge badge-info">3</span> Custom Style Guides & RAG Embeddings
      </h2>
      <p style="color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7;">
        Teams can customize their code review guidelines using our Markdown editor.
      </p>
      <ul style="margin: 12px 0 12px 24px; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8;">
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Vector Storage in pgvector:</strong> When you save your style guide, GitPR parses each rule section, generates 768-dimensional vector embeddings, and stores them in PostgreSQL with pgvector.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Strict Multi-Tenant Isolation:</strong> Every vector similarity query strictly filters by <code>installation_id</code>. Your custom style guide rules are completely private to your GitHub account and can never be retrieved or viewed by other accounts.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4 -->
    <section style="border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); padding-top: 28px;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 1.3rem; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; color: var(--text-primary);">
        <span class="badge badge-warning">4</span> Complete Data Deletion on Uninstall
      </h2>
      <p style="color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7;">
        You have complete control over your data. If you decide to uninstall GitPR from your GitHub account:
      </p>
      <ul style="margin: 12px 0 12px 24px; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8;">
        <li>GitHub sends an automated <code>installation.deleted</code> webhook to our server.</li>
        <li>GitPR immediately and permanently deletes the installation row from PostgreSQL.</li>
        <li>Through cascading database foreign keys (<code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code>), all associated records are permanently wiped:
          <ul style="margin: 6px 0 6px 20px;">
            <li>Encrypted API keys and initialization vectors</li>
            <li>Custom style guide markdown and pgvector embeddings</li>
            <li>Review logs, latency telemetry, and comment outcome history</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>No residual tenant data is retained.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 5 -->
    <section style="border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); padding-top: 28px;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 1.3rem; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; color: var(--text-primary);">
        <span class="badge badge-info">5</span> Web Security & Authorization
      </h2>
      <ul style="margin: 12px 0 12px 24px; color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8;">
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">GitHub OAuth & Server-Side Sessions:</strong> Authentication is handled strictly via GitHub OAuth. Session cookies are stored server-side with <code>httpOnly</code>, <code>secure</code>, and <code>sameSite=lax</code> attributes.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">CSRF Protection:</strong> All state-changing endpoints (saving keys, deleting keys, updating style guides) require cryptographic CSRF token validation.</li>
        <li><strong style="color: var(--text-primary);">Multi-Tenant Guard:</strong> The server verifies that the requested <code>installation_id</code> is present in the signed-in user's authorized installation list before serving settings or metrics. Requests with tampered installation IDs are rejected with <code>403 Forbidden</code>.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>

<%- include('partials/footer') %>
